# 59-gs-vertex-ring: one persistent vertex ring for the GS draw batches.
#
# Problem: every batch re-specified its vertices with glBufferData. On the Intel HD 4400 driver that reallocates the
# store and blocks on the GPU; draw submission cost ~0.71 s of every 5 s at 0.5x (387 batches/frame, title/attract).
#
# Change: one 32 MB vertex buffer, glBufferStorage + persistent coherent map when the context has OpenGL 4.4
# (GL_DYNAMIC_DRAW is not a valid BufferStorage flag: GL_DYNAMIC_STORAGE_BIT 0x0100 is), else a plain BufferData ring.
# Attributes move to ARB_vertex_attrib_binding (VertexAttribFormat/Binding) so a batch only needs glBindVertexBuffer
# with its ring offset. A ring wrap waits on a fence taken at the previous wrap. GOW_GS_HW_RING=0 keeps the old path.
#
# Also clamps PS2X_GS_HW_SCALE to 0.25..4 instead of std::max(1.0f, ...): on the 471c831 baseline any scale below 1x
# silently stayed at 1x, so the launcher's low-spec profiles could not lower the internal resolution.
#
# Measured (i3-4170 + HD 4400, Asia disc, title/attract loop, VU1 x64 JIT, 5 s windows, 0.5x internal):
#   baseline       guest 52.5-53.0 fps, gs_ms 2130-2200
#   59 (this)      guest 52.6-55.8 fps, gs_ms 1940-2130 (draw submission ~0.55 s/5 s, was ~0.71)
#   draws discarded guest 59.9 fps -> the remaining wall is VU1 (~92% of a core), not the renderer.
#   0.35x internal  guest unchanged (53.8-56.6): the remaining cost is per batch, not per pixel.
#
# Tried and not kept (measured slower or neutral on this driver, details in the 2026-11-04 renderer notes):
#   std140 uniform block per batch, GL state cache early-outs, palette atlas across CLUT loads, canonical state
#   comparison, TEXFLUSH as a no-op.
